    rationale: `This tests understanding of MULTIMODAL PAIN MANAGEMENT and OPIOID SAFETY CONCERNS post-operatively. Clinical reasoning: (1) This patient: Pain 6/10 (moderate), received morphine 2 hours ago, already drowsy, RR 14 (normal is 12-20, but low side suggests opioid effect). (2) Additional opioid now would: (a) Risk increasing drowsiness. (b) Risk respiratory depression (RR drops further). (c) Maybe not needed if non-opioid options not yet tried. (3) Multimodal approach: (a) Opioids are part of post-op pain management, but NOT the only tool. (b) Non-opioid options: (i) Acetaminophen 650-1000 mg q6h (safe, effective, doesn't suppress respiration). (ii) NSAIDs (ibuprofen 400-600 mg q6h, naproxen 250-500 mg)—if no contraindications. (iii) Regional anesthesia (spinal/epidural if patient has it). (iv) Non-pharmacologic: Splinting incision (supports it, reduces pain with movement), ice packs, positioning, distraction. (c) Combine approaches. (4) Timing: Morphine given 2 hours ago. If it's a short-acting dose (e.g., 5 mg IV), peak effect ~30 min, wearing off by 2 hours. So patient could receive another dose, BUT should first try non-opioids. (5) RR 14: Low-normal. If additional opioid given, must monitor that RR doesn't drop further (goal >10, <8 concerning for hypoventilation). (6) Assessment approach: "Your pain is 6/10, which is better than before but not ideal. Let's try acetaminophen and ibuprofen together, and some positioning changes. If that doesn't help, we can give more morphine. But because you're already drowsy, I want to try the other options first to keep you safer." (7) Option A (just accept): WRONG. Pain 6/10 should be managed. (8) Option C (drowsiness is normal): While some drowsiness is expected, severe drowsiness + low RR is concerning. Should assess before giving more. (9) Option D (addicted): After 2 hours post-op, patient is NOT addicted. She's appropriately receiving opioids for post-op pain. This confuses addiction with appropriate pain management. (10) Option B is correct.`,
