    rationale: `This tests understanding of INADEQUATE PAIN CONTROL assessment and escalation strategies. Clinical reasoning: (1) This patient: Pain 9/10 (SEVERE), already received morphine 8 mg 1.5 hours ago, still in severe pain. Vital signs stable, so NOT in hypovolemic shock. (2) Differential for severe persistent pain post-op: (a) Inadequate opioid dose for this patient (pain perception varies). (b) Inadequate opioid dosing interval (8 mg morphine IV lasts ~2-4 hours; may need repeat dose). (c) Surgical complication: Hematoma (bleeding in surgical bed causing pain/pressure), infection (unlikely at 8 hours but possible), urinary retention (patient likely has urinary catheter post-prostate surgery, but blockage/retention can occur). (d) Opioid tolerance (if patient chronically on opioids pre-op). (3) This is NOT "opioid-seeking" behavior. Opioid-seeking is when patient requests opioids WITHOUT a valid medical reason, or lies about pain, or uses drugs recreationally. This patient had MAJOR pelvic surgery and is reporting severe pain—very credible. (4) Assessment: (a) How much morphine was given initially? Was it reduced dose? (b) Is patient responding at all to morphine (drowsy, any improvement)? (c) Location of pain—incision vs. perineum vs. bladder? (d) Any signs of hematoma (severe pain + swelling)? (e) Bladder functioning (Foley patent, draining, or distended)? (d) Fever? (e) Any abdominal distension/rigidity? (5) Management: (a) ESCALATE: Give additional opioid (morphine 4-8 mg IV repeat if within safe interval). (b) ADD NON-OPIOIDS: Acetaminophen 1000 mg IV/PO, NSAIDs if safe. (c) CONSIDER ALTERNATIVE ROUTES: IV is fastest; PO for maintenance. (d) DISCUSS EPIDURAL/PCA: For major pelvic surgery, epidural or patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) may provide better pain control than intermittent IV dosing. (e) INVESTIGATE COMPLICATIONS: Palpate surgical site (hematoma?), ensure Foley is patent (urinary retention?), check temp (infection?). (6) Communication: "Your pain is very high even after morphine. This tells me we need to do more to help. Let me give you additional pain medication, add some other pain-relieving drugs, and we should talk about better ways to keep your pain controlled—like an epidural or a pump you can control." (7) Option A (opioid-seeking): WRONG judgment. Severe pain post-prostate surgery is real and expected. (8) Option C (wait): WRONG. Severe pain should not be ignored. (9) Option D (benzodiazepines): WRONG. Benzodiazepines are not analgesics. They don't treat pain, though they may mask patient's awareness. Not appropriate here. (10) Option B is correct.`,
