    rationale: `This tests understanding of OPIOID TOLERANCE and pain management in chronically opioid-taking patients. Clinical reasoning: (1) This patient: Chronic oxycodone user (15 mg BID = 30 mg daily). This is a SIGNIFICANT dose. (2) Opioid tolerance: With chronic opioid use, body adapts. Requires HIGHER doses to achieve same pain relief. (3) Post-op opioid dosing typically calculated for opioid-NAIVE patients. Standard morphine 10 mg IV in naive patient is analgesic. In tolerant patient, 10 mg morphine may be subtherapeutic. (4) Why she's not drowsy: Tolerance means she's not getting the opioid effects (both analgesia AND side effects like drowsiness). She's subtherapeutically dosed. (5) Pain 7/10 post-total knee replacement: Definitely not controlled, especially in a tolerant patient. (6) Management: (a) INCREASE OPIOID DOSE: Give morphine 10-15 mg IV (higher dose for tolerant patient). (b) Consider STRONGER OPIOID: Hydromorphone (Dilaudid) is more potent, may work better. Dose ~1.5 mg IV equivalent to 10 mg morphine, but more potent in tolerant patients. (c) USE MULTIMODAL: Add non-opioids (acetaminophen, NSAIDs). (d) EXPLAIN TO PATIENT: "Your body is used to higher opioid doses from your home medication. We'll increase your dose to match. This isn't addiction—it's how we manage pain in patients already on chronic opioids." (e) POST-OP PLANNING: When discharged, transition back to oxycodone. Don't abruptly stop IV opioids (risk of withdrawal). May need higher oxycodone dose post-op than pre-op for several days, then taper back to baseline. (7) Difference: TOLERANCE (requires more opioid for same effect) vs. ADDICTION (compulsive use despite harm) vs. DEPENDENCE (physical adaptation; physical withdrawal if stopped abruptly). This patient has tolerance and dependence (expected with chronic opioids), not addiction. (8) Option A (exaggerating): WRONG. Tolerance is real and expected. (9) Option C (cannot give more): WRONG logic. Tolerance means she NEEDS more. (10) Option D (home oxycodone sufficient): WRONG. She's not taking oxycodone during hospitalization. Switched to IV morphine. And morphine 10 mg is insufficient for her tolerance. (11) Option B is correct.`,
